<h1>Core concepts</h1>
<p class="lede">Everything in Booster Auth fits a layered structure: <b>tenant</b> → <b>user pool</b> → the pool's <b>applications</b>,
    <b>users</b> and <b>permissions</b>. After this page you will know what every screen in the console is for.</p>

<ul class="tree" aria-label="A tenant, a pool and what lives inside a pool">
    <li><span class="node t"><b>Tenant</b><i>your organisation or one of your customers</i></span>
        <ul>
            <li><span class="node p"><b>User pool</b><i>an audience of users and its rules</i></span>
                <ul>
                    <li><span class="node"><b>Applications</b><i>clients · web, mobile, APIs that sign in with this pool</i></span></li>
                    <li><span class="node"><b>Users, service accounts</b><i>the members of the pool</i></span></li>
                    <li><span class="node"><b>Roles, scopes, authorization schema</b><i>who can do what</i></span></li>
                    <li><span class="node"><b>Identity providers</b><i>LDAP / AD, Google, Microsoft, SAML…</i></span></li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="tenant">Tenant</h2>
<p>A tenant is the top-level container. You create a tenant for your own organisation; if you serve several customers, you can create one
    tenant per customer. Its only setting is a name; its real job is to group pools and control who can manage them.</p>
<ul>
    <li>The person who creates a tenant becomes its <b>owner</b>.</li>
    <li>On the <b>Tenant Access</b> screen you grant other administrators Owner / Manager / Viewer rights.</li>
    <li>Deleting a tenant deletes all its pools.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="pool">User pool</h2>
<p>A pool is an <b>audience of users</b> plus the <b>rules</b> that apply to it. Create separate pools for “Customers” and “Employees” and their
    users, sessions, password rules and permissions stay fully isolated. The same e-mail address can be two different accounts in two pools.</p>
<p>What you manage at pool level:</p>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sign-in policy</td><td>Sign-in methods (password, e-mail code, SMS code, external identity providers), user identifier (e-mail, username, phone), failed attempts and lockout.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Password policy</td><td>Minimum length; upper / lower case, digits, special characters; expiry and history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multi-factor authentication</td><td>A second step with an e-mail or SMS code after password sign-in; can be required.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session policy</td><td>Access token (default 15 min) and refresh token (default 14 days) lifetimes, idle timeout.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Registration policy</td><td>Anyone can sign up or invitation only; e-mail / phone verification; account creation on first external sign-in.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Default roles and scopes</td><td>Granted automatically to newly registered users.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Redirect domain policy</td><td>Which domains the pool's applications may register redirect URIs on.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="identity-providers">Identity providers</h2>
<p>The pool's <b>Identity Providers</b> screen lets users sign in with another identity: Google, Microsoft, Apple, LinkedIn, Facebook, GitHub, X,
    any OpenID Connect provider and SAML. <b>LDAP / Active Directory</b> is added on the same screen; employees then sign in on the normal form with
    their directory username and password, the account is created on first sign-in and passwords stay in the directory.</p>

<h2 id="application">Application (client)</h2>
<p>An application is your software that signs users in or calls APIs through Booster Auth: a website, a single-page app (SPA), a mobile app or a
    server-side service. Each application belongs to <b>exactly one pool</b> and works with that pool's users.</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Client key:</b> the OIDC <code>client_id</code> your application uses.</li>
    <li><b>Grant types:</b> Authorization Code (user sign-in), Refresh Token, Client Credentials (machine access with a service account), Token Exchange.</li>
    <li><b>Redirect URIs:</b> where users return after sign-in; must match exactly. Post-logout URIs are defined separately.</li>
    <li><b>Allowed APIs (audience) and scopes:</b> which APIs the application can get tokens for and which scopes it can request.</li>
    <li><b>Consent, status and logo:</b> whether users are asked for consent, whether the application is active, and the logo on the sign-in screen.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p><b>No client secret.</b> Applications that sign users in are protected with PKCE; server-to-server access uses the key and
    secret of a <b>service account</b>.</p></div>

<h2 id="users">Users and service accounts</h2>
<p><b>Users</b> are the members of a pool. They can sign up themselves, be added and invited by an administrator, or be imported in bulk from CSV.
    <b>Service accounts</b> are non-human machine identities: a background job or another system gets tokens with its key and secret.</p>

<h2 id="authorization">Roles, scopes and the authorization schema</h2>
<p>Booster Auth offers two complementary authorization models:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Roles and scopes</b> (per pool): e.g. an <code>admin</code> role or an <code>orders:read</code> scope. They are written into the token as
        <code>role</code> and <code>scope</code>; your application decides by looking at the token. Enough for most applications.</li>
    <li><b>Authorization schema</b> (relationship-based, ReBAC): for record-level questions such as “can Ali edit this document in the Sales folder?”.
        You define resource types (Document, Folder), the links between them and permissions; your application asks the AuthZ API.</li>
</ul>
<p>Details: <a href="/en/docs/authorization">Authorization checks</a>.</p>

<h2 id="management">The management pool and console permissions</h2>
<p>The <b>Booster</b> tenant and the <b>Booster Auth</b> pool created during installation are for the admin console itself. Console administrators
    live in this pool, and it does not count toward license limits. Administrators get rights on tenants, pools and applications in the order
    <b>Owner › Manager › Viewer</b> on the related “Access” screens.</p>
